n/math-academy-id "q-25238" :question/problem "If the expression ${x}^{2}+4x+5$ is written as $(x+p{)}^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+2{)}^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+2{)}^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=2$ and $q=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323842 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d85fc46-850a-5c84-b5f9-35452de37bcd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321370} {:answer/id #uuid "44269fc8-5ddc-5684-9a2c-f2df92134197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321684} {:answer/id #uuid "94c0a1e3-7755-553c-8ed6-117195d88b56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322330} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be16ea-f2f9-542f-96e8-bd3c135b2b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6{)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322803} {:answer/id #uuid "fccccf58-378d-5bfc-9fa6-35c13dd2fd88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44269fc8-5ddc-5684-9a2c-f2df92134197" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+6{)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321684} :answer-field/id #uuid "701aa2ec-89fe-5586-b37e-c29f85e40b20" :answer-field/k