he trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${13}^{2},$ writing the constant term $176$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 13y+{13}^{2}}-\\,{13}^{2}+176\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+13)^{2}-{13}^{2}+176\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+13)^{2}+7.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=13$ and $q=7.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "597e36b5-f1c0-5227-ac21-11afe821395b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511790} {:answer/id #uuid "7adfd77a-2c35-5839-aba3-20025a57f8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511799} {:answer/id #uuid "8f6f60e3-d6aa-5bae-a63d-aad7f1fb4f10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=12,\\,q=-85" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511803} {:answer/id #uuid "94093a8e-c60a-58df-9727-f636c70bb6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=59" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511804} {:answer/id #uuid "fe944237-c002-5cd8-adbb-4450570fa880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7adfd77a-2c35-5839-aba3-20025a57f8bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "p=6,\\,q=23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511799} :answer-field/id #uuid "6496e219-3ebb-5bd6-854b-857232ef2553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ecf84f0e-d57b-5f53-a7ed-0546a4b066ac" :question/problem "If the expression ${y}^{2}+12y+59$ is written as $(y+p{)}^{2}+q,$ then what are the values of $p$ and $q?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+59\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $59$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 6y+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+59\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+6{)}^{2}-{6}^{2}+59\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+6{)}^{2}+23.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=6$ and $q=23.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "366b36a5-c909-5ea5-a05d-a6052fcd41d7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eccc2b9-d4b6-5464-bcb2-f11dfffbefd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511796}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6eccc2b9-d4b6-5464-bcb2-f11dfffbefd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511796} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e0d8cc4-108f-5156-8662-da6724bb975f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511775} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d0d0870-2885-500a-86bf-9400eec886d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d0d0870-2885-500a-86bf-9400eec886d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511777} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0238128-83ed-5e2d-b795-f0c15c5c49ef" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f4676eb4-e2e0-54fb-b3e0-e8177cd75226" :question/problem "If the expression ${z}^{2}+24z+138$ is written as $(z+p)^{2}+q$, then what are the values of $p$ and $q$?\n\n$p =$\n{{field-1}},\n$q =$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+138\n$$\n\nNow, here's the trick. We *add* and *then subtract* ${12}^{2},$ writing the constant term $138$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 12z+{12}^{2}}-\\,{12}^{2}+138\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}-{12}^{2}+138\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+12)^{2}-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p=12$ and $q=-6.$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on a Quadratic Expression"}]}
